There, upon the <s>kūrca</s> (bundle of <s>kuśa</s> grass) consisting of ten <s>kalā</s>s (aspects), uttering the prolonged <s>oṃkāra</s>, one should place the <s>kumbha</s> (ritual vessel). The <s>kumbha</s> is made of gold and other [metals]. As stated —
'One of gold, or of silver, or of clay, as prescribed;
One should purify the <s>kumbha</s> thoroughly with the <s>astra-mantra</s> (weapon-mantra), O <s>Sureśvarī</s> (Goddess of the gods).' Thus.

And thus in the <s>Nāradīya</s>:
'One should not converse with women or <s>śūdra</s>s; at night one should not be intent on recitation;
one should not recite at the <s>sandhyā</s> (twilight) times, nor at dusk, nor at both [twilights];
the performer of rites should use cloth and pure water brought by a <s>brāhmaṇa</s>.' Thus. || KrdC_5.2 ||

Thus, what is stated in the <s>Puraścaraṇa-candrikā</s> we consider proper:
'For <s>brāhmaṇa</s>s unable to perform <s>homa</s>, the recitation is doubled;
for the other <s>varṇa</s>s, it is prescribed as tripled.' Thus.

The fifth is cloud-colored — these are the best among cows.
The fourth [variant] is reddish; the sixth [also]; the seventh is hoof-reddish;
the eighth is <s>kapilā</s> — among cows, this should be known as the <s>kapilā</s> group. Thus it is stated. || KrdC_7.97 ||
